601,1659,1661],{},[241,1655,1656,1658],{},[18,1657,993],{}," doesn't evaluate the next case's condition",[18,1660,993],{}," jumps to the next case's body unconditionally — it doesn't check the next case's value. This is unlike C.",[1512,1663,1664,1672,1673,1676,1677,1680],{},[241,1665,1666,1668,1669],{},[18,1667,1552],{}," over a string yields ",[18,1670,1671],{},"(byte_offset, rune)",": the index is the byte position, not the rune index. For \"世a\", the first iteration gives ",[18,1674,1675],{},"(0, '世')",", the second gives ",[18,1678,1679],{},"(3, 'a')"," — the 3 is the byte offset after the 3-byte \"世\".",[1512,1682,1683,1688,1689,998],{},[241,1684,1685,1687],{},[18,1686,1552],{}," over a map is unordered",": map iteration order is unspecified (and intentionally randomized in Go). If you need ordered keys, sort them: ",[18,1690,1691],{},"var keys []string; for k := range m { keys = append(keys, k) }; sort.Strings(keys)",[1512,1693,1694,1601,1697,1700,1701,1703,1704,1707,1708,1711],{},[241,1695,1696],{},"Modifying a slice during range",[18,1698,1699],{},"for i, v := range s { s = append(s, v) }"," — ",[18,1702,1552],{}," evaluates ",[18,1705,1706],{},"s"," once at loop start, so the appended elements aren't iterated. But modifying existing elements (",[18,1709,1710],{},"s[i] = ...",") works.",[1512,1713,1714,1601,1724,1721,1726,1728,1729,1731,1732,1734,1735,1737],{},[241,1715,1716,1718,1719,1721,1722],{},[18,1717,1575],{}," in a ",[18,1720,30],{}," inside a ",[18,1723,24],{},[18,1725,1575],{},[18,1727,30],{}," continues the enclosing ",[18,1730,24],{}," loop, not the ",[18,1733,30],{}," (there's no loop iteration in ",[18,1736,30],{},"). Subtle but correct.",[1512,1739,1740,1601,1745,1748,1749,1752,1753,1755],{},[241,1741,1742,1744],{},[18,1743,34],{}," can't jump over variable declarations",[18,1746,1747],{},"goto end; x := 5; end:"," is an error (jumping over ",[18,1750,1751],{},"x","'s declaration). ",[18,1754,34],{}," is restricted to avoid spaghetti.",[1512,1757,1758,1761],{},[241,1759,1760],{},"Loop variable capture (pre-1.22)",": the classic goroutine-in-a-loop bug. Go 1.22 fixes it; on older Go, pass the variable as an argument or use a local copy.",[45,1763,1765],{"id":1764},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1767,1768],{},"A developer runs this on Go 1.21 and expects \"0 1 2\" but gets \"3 3 3\":",[50,1770,1771],{"language":52},[54,1772,1774],{"className":56,"code":1773,"language":52,"meta":58,"style":58},"for i := 0; i \u003C 3; i++ {\n    go func() {\n        fmt.Println(i)\n    }()\n}\ntime.Sleep(time.Second)\n",[18,1775,1776,1798,1807,1816,1821,1825],{"__ignoreMap":58},[62,1777,1778,1780,1782,1784,1786,1788,1790,1792,1794,1796],{"class":64,"line":65},[62,1779,24],{"class":68},[62,1781,284],{"class":71},[62,1783,191],{"class":68},[62,1785,79],{"class":78},[62,1787,291],{"class":71},[62,1789,119],{"class":68},[62,1791,726],{"class":78},[62,1793,299],{"class":71},[62,1795,302],{"class":68},[62,1797,82],{"class":71},[62,1799,1800,1802,1804],{"class":64,"line":85},[62,1801,737],{"class":68},[62,1803,740],{"class":68},[62,1805,1806],{"class":71},"() {\n",[62,1808,1809,1812,1814],{"class":64,"line":105},[62,1810,1811],{"class":71},"        fmt.",[62,1813,92],{"class":91},[62,1815,313],{"class":71},[62,1817,1818],{"class":64,"line":126},[62,1819,1820],{"class":71},"    }()\n",[62,1822,1823],{"class":64,"line":140},[62,1824,166],{"class":71},[62,1826,1827,1829,1832],{"class":64,"line":149},[62,1828,1346],{"class":71},[62,1830,1831],{"class":91},"Sleep",[62,1833,1834],{"class":71},"(time.Second)\n",[1836,1837,1838,1842,1862,1868,1874,1933,1936],"details",{},[1839,1840,1841],"summary",{},"Answer",[14,1843,1844,1845,1848,1849,1852,1853,1855,1856,1858,1859,1861],{},"Pre-Go 1.22, the loop variable ",[18,1846,1847],{},"i"," is a ",[241,1850,1851],{},"single variable"," reused across iterations — not a fresh variable per iteration. All three goroutines capture the same ",[18,1854,1847],{}," by reference. By the time they run (after the ",[18,1857,1831],{},"), the loop has finished and ",[18,1860,1847],{}," is 3 — so all three print 3.",[14,1863,1864,1865,1867],{},"Go 1.22+ fixes this: each iteration gets a distinct ",[18,1866,1847],{},", so the goroutines print 0, 1, 2.",[14,1869,1870,1871,1873],{},"The fix (portable across versions) — pass ",[18,1872,1847],{}," as an argument to the goroutine, creating a fresh copy per call:",[54,1875,1877],{"className":56,"code":1876,"language":52,"meta":58,"style":58},"for i := 0; i \u003C 3; i++ {\n    go func(i int) {\n        fmt.Println(i)\n    }(i)\n}\n",[18,1878,1879,1901,1916,1924,1929],{"__ignoreMap":58},[62,1880,1881,1883,1885,1887,1889,1891,1893,1895,1897,1899],{"class":64,"line":65},[62,1882,24],{"class":68},[62,1884,284],{"class":71},[62,1886,191],{"class":68},[62,1888,79],{"class":78},[62,1890,291],{"class":71},[62,1892,119],{"class":68},[62,1894,726],{"class":78},[62,1896,299],{"class":71},[62,1898,302],{"class":68},[62,1900,82],{"class":71},[62,1902,1903,1905,1907,1909,1912,1914],{"class":64,"line":85},[62,1904,737],{"class":68},[62,1906,740],{"class":68},[62,1908,95],{"class":71},[62,1910,1847],{"class":1911},"sCrzJ",[62,1913,1034],{"class":68},[62,1915,1026],{"class":71},[62,1917,1918,1920,1922],{"class":64,"line":105},[62,1919,1811],{"class":71},[62,1921,92],{"class":91},[62,1923,313],{"class":71},[62,1925,1926],{"class":64,"line":126},[62,1927,1928],{"class":71},"    }(i)\n",[62,1930,1931],{"class":64,"line":140},[62,1932,166],{"class":71},[14,1934,1935],{},"::\nOr, on 1.22+, just use the original — the fix is automatic.",[14,1937,1938,1941,1942,1945],{},[241,1939,1940],{},"The lesson",": pre-1.22, loop variables are shared across iterations; closures\u002Fgoroutines capture them by reference and see the final value. Pass the variable as an argument (",[18,1943,1944],{},"func(i int) { ... }(i)",") to create a fresh copy per iteration. Go 1.22+ makes each iteration's variable distinct, eliminating the bug.",[45,1947,1948],{"id":1839},"Summary",[14,1950,1951,1952,21,1955,1957,1958,1960,1961,1963,1964,1498,1966,1968,1969,1971],{},"You can now use ",[18,1953,1954],{},"if init; cond",[18,1956,24],{}," (all forms including ",[18,1959,1552],{}," over integers in 1.22+), ",[18,1962,27],{}," (no default fallthrough, no-expression form, type switch), ",[18,1965,1503],{},[18,1967,1575],{}," with labels, and ",[18,1970,30],{},". You understand the Go 1.22 loop-variable fix and the mandatory-brace \u002F same-line-brace rules. Next: arrays, slices, and strings — the most-used data structures.",[1973,1974,1975],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .svdQ7, html code.shiki .svdQ7{--shiki-default:#D73A49;--shiki-github-dark:#F97583}html pre.shiki code .ssxIu, html code.shiki .ssxIu{--shiki-default:#24292E;--shiki-github-dark:#E1E4E8}html pre.shiki code .snvgF, html code.shiki .snvgF{--shiki-default:#005CC5;--shiki-github-dark:#79B8FF}html pre.shiki code .sIsaT, html code.shiki .sIsaT{--shiki-default:#6F42C1;--shiki-github-dark:#B392F0}html pre.shiki code .sJ6F3, html code.shiki .sJ6F3{--shiki-default:#032F62;--shiki-github-dark:#9ECBFF}html pre.shiki code .sdCPZ, html code.shiki .sdCPZ{--shiki-default:#6A737D;--shiki-github-dark:#6A737D}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html.github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);backgr